-A learned Bloom filter answers set membership queries within a space budget by splitting memory between a model and backup filters. Methods such as PLBF and Gama choose this split to minimize the false positive (FP) rate of a single query. Real query streams, however, repeat their absent keys, and a static filter pays for an FP every time a falsely accepted key returns. A small negative cache removes these repeats, but it competes with the filter for the same memory, and its value depends on which keys the filter lets through. We pose the following problem: given a learning prefix of the stream and one memory budget, how should the budget be split among the model, the backup filters and the cache so that the rest of the stream produces as few FPs as possible? A cache changes what each filter bit is worth, yet the problem keeps PLBF's structure. For a fixed cache, the optimal segment FPRs have PLBF's form once each segment's negative mass is replaced by the number of its queries that the cache will miss, so the FastPLBF optimizer can be reused unchanged. Our method, RA-PLBF, estimates these misses from per-key counts of the learning prefix, ranks every candidate split without building it, and re-scores a short list from actual bucket loads. Without repeats it returns Gama's structure exactly. On four datasets and four packet traces, RA-PLBF matches exhaustive replay of the learning prefix in quality, has 2--16\% fewer FPs on a trace held out until after the code freeze, and selects 2--5$\times$ faster, up to 49$\times$ at 100 million learning queries.
