 \paragraph{Approach.} Our allocator \ra addresses them in turn. For C1, it replaces the negative mass $h_i$ of each PLBF segment by $M_i$, the number of the segment's queries that the cache is expected \emph{not} to absorb, and hands these weights to the FastPLBF allocator. For C2, it computes $M_i$ from per-key counts of the learning prefix with Che's characteristic-time approximation~\cite{che2002,fricker2012}, a Poisson correction for set-associative buckets, and Good--Turing estimates for low counts and unseen keys~\cite{good1953}. For C3, it ranks all candidates with this model, builds only the five best, and re-scores them from the actual bucket assignment of their false positives. When no key repeats, every step reduces to Gama. The result is a selector that reads the learning trace once and reuses its counts for every candidate, whereas replay must scan the trace once per candidate.
 
-\paragraph{Results.} We evaluate on four datasets and four packet traces under physical byte accounting. \emph{Re-weighting.} Against RA-global, which tunes the cache share on Gama's weights, re-weighting reduces FPs in 12 of 13 real-trace cases (geometric mean 0.935). On a pre-declared trace evaluated once after the code freeze, \ra has 2--16\% fewer FPs than replay, and fewer than the hindsight best of replay's candidates, which a cache share chosen on top of Gama's weights cannot reach. \emph{Selection.} Against exhaustive learning-period replay, the strongest baseline, \ra has no more total FPs in 24 of 30 settings; across 21 clusters its geometric mean is 0.975 of replay's. It selects 1.7--5.4$\times$ faster at default settings, and the gap widens with the learning period: from 1M to 100M learning queries, replay's selection time grows from 7\,s to 480\,s and \ra's from 3\,s to 10\,s, a 49$\times$ speedup. \emph{Baselines.} \ra beats a plain BF with a replay-tuned cache in 718 of 748 cases, the 30 losses being adversarial queries that never repeat. It has up to 54$\times$ fewer FPs than Gama and reduces to Gama exactly on all 140 no-repeat cases.
+\paragraph{Results.} We evaluate on four datasets and four packet traces under physical byte accounting. \emph{Re-weighting.} Against RA-global, which tunes the cache share on Gama's weights, re-weighting reduces FPs in 12 of 13 real-trace cases (geometric mean 0.935). On a pre-declared trace evaluated once after the code freeze, \ra has 2--16\% fewer FPs than replay, and fewer than the hindsight best of replay's candidates, which a cache share chosen on top of Gama's weights cannot reach. \emph{Selection.} Against exhaustive learning-period replay, the strongest baseline, \ra has no more total FPs in 24 of 30 settings; across 21 clusters its geometric mean is 0.975 of replay's. This aggregate FP advantage is not significant in the two-sided clustered test ($p=0.096$); the main result against Replay is lower selection cost with similar observed FP counts, not universal FP superiority. It selects 1.7--5.4$\times$ faster at default settings, and the gap widens with the learning period: from 1M to 100M learning queries, replay's selection time grows from 7\,s to 480\,s and \ra's from 3\,s to 10\,s, a 49$\times$ speedup. \emph{Baselines.} \ra beats a plain BF with a replay-tuned cache in 718 of 748 cases, the 30 losses being adversarial queries that never repeat. It has up to 54$\times$ fewer FPs than Gama and reduces to Gama exactly on all 140 no-repeat cases.
 
 \paragraph{Contributions.} The contributions of this paper are as follows.
 \begin{itemize}
   \item \emph{Problem.} We formalize repeat-aware memory allocation for LBFs with a negative cache under physical accounting, with PLBF and Gama as special cases (\S\ref{sec:problem}).
   \item \emph{Cache-aware allocation.} We model, per score segment, the FPs a set-associative cache cannot absorb, and prove that, for a fixed cache and characteristic time, the optimal segment FPRs keep PLBF's form with these weights, so the FastPLBF optimizer becomes cache-aware unchanged; the gain over tuning only the cache share is the ratio of arithmetic to geometric mean of the regions' miss ratios (\S\ref{sec:model}, \S\ref{sec:analysis}).
   \item \emph{Scalable selection.} We give \ra, which summarizes the learning trace once into per-key counts, ranks all candidates by the model and re-scores a short list from actual bucket loads; its selection cost does not grow with the number of trace scans that replay needs. A compact verified-prefix cache, and an index-free fingerprint variant, complete the structure (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:problem}).
-  \item \emph{Evaluation.} On 1,146 cases and a pre-declared holdout trace, re-weighting beats tuning only the cache share on real traces, and \ra matches or beats exhaustive replay at a fraction of its selection cost, up to 49$\times$ less at 100M learning queries, and beats Gama, the official Gama code on its own data and a plain BF with a cache (\S\ref{sec:exp}).
+  \item \emph{Evaluation.} On 1,146 cases and a pre-declared holdout trace, we separately measure workload-dependent FP gains from re-weighting and lower candidate-selection cost than exhaustive replay, up to 49$\times$ faster at 100M learning queries with similar observed FP counts. Comparisons with Gama, its official implementation and a plain BF with a cache expose both improvements and limitations (\S\ref{sec:exp}).
 \end{itemize}
