+The SQLite experiment uses a batched timing protocol: the native filter/cache loop first processes the query batch using known membership labels for negative-cache feedback, and real SQL point lookups are then executed for the accepted queries. The timer covers both phases, but this is not a strictly interleaved online implementation in which SQL results drive each subsequent insertion. Under this batched metric, with file-backed SQLite (about 3--4\,$\mu$s per unfiltered access), \ra uses 39--65\% fewer backend accesses and 0.47--0.72$\times$ Gama's combined time on the traces at 24\,KiB, and 0.77$\times$ on Shalla. On URL 128\,KiB it is 1.7$\times$ slower (2.1$\times$ with an in-memory backend), saving only 52 backend accesses per 200,000 queries while using three times as many trees. On CAIDA 256\,KiB, its measured time is 3.165\,$\mu$s per query versus 4.004\,$\mu$s without a filter; on COD 1024\,KiB it is 4.181 versus 4.096\,$\mu$s. The fixed 48-tree FastPLBF baseline is faster than the unfiltered path on both. These measurements must not be interpreted as strictly interleaved backend-feedback latency; that experiment remains to be performed.
 
