 \subsection{When to use RA-PLBF}
 \label{sec:limits}
 
-\ra pays off when absent keys repeat and the model separates members from negatives. Three conditions shape its use. First, every candidate contains a model. Where the model is weak, as on the TCP-only traces, or where queries never repeat, as in the fresh adversarial workload, a plain BF with a cache is the better choice; a zero-tree candidate would let the selector make that choice itself. Second, the prefix cache relies on a member-hash index at the backend, outside the filter budget (8 bytes per member, 316\,KiB on MAWI-21 against a 24\,KiB filter); without backend access, the fingerprint variant applies at 5\% more FPs. Third, \ra minimizes FP counts on a stationary stream. Where query latency dominates (URL 128) or traffic drifts, the objective should include query cost, or the selection should be repeated as the stream changes. Construction takes 2.8--24\,s more than the official Gama code on six of eight settings (Table~\ref{tab:e2e}).
