 \section{Conclusion}
 \label{sec:conclusion}
 
-We presented an approach that splits one memory budget among a learned model, its segment filters and a negative cache for query streams in which absent keys repeat. It was shown that a cache changes what a filter bit is worth, and that for a fixed cache the optimal segment FPRs keep PLBF's form once each segment's negative mass is replaced by the queries the cache will miss. \ra turns this into a selector that summarizes the learning period once, ranks every candidate without building it, and re-scores a short list from actual bucket loads; without repeats it returns Gama's structure exactly. On four datasets and four packet traces it matches exhaustive replay in quality, wins on a trace held out until after the code freeze, and selects up to 49$\times$ faster. Future work includes measuring how popularity correlates with the score in real workloads, which Proposition~\ref{prop:gain} turns into a predicted gain, a zero-tree candidate, query cost in the objective, and re-selection under drift.
+We presented an approach that splits one memory budget among a learned model, its segment filters and a negative cache for query streams in which absent keys repeat. It was shown that a cache changes what a filter bit is worth, and that for a fixed cache the optimal segment FPRs keep PLBF's form once each segment's negative mass is replaced by the queries the cache will miss. \ra turns this into a selector that summarizes the learning period once, ranks every candidate without building it, and re-scores a short list from actual bucket loads; without repeats it returns Gama's structure exactly. On four datasets and four packet traces it has similar observed FP counts to exhaustive learning-period replay while selecting up to 49$\times$ faster. Re-weighting yields additional FP reductions on some workloads, including a trace held out until after the code freeze, but the aggregate FP advantage is not significant in the clustered test and does not establish statistical equivalence. Future work includes measuring how popularity correlates with the score in real workloads, which Proposition~\ref{prop:gain} turns into a predicted gain, a zero-tree candidate, query cost in the objective, and re-selection under drift.
